{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 36, "model_name": "mistralai/ministral-14b-instruct-2512", "content": "Raymond III was a count of Tripoli who ruled during the late 12th and early 13th centuries. He was the son of Raymond II and his wife Hodierna who was the daughter of Bohemond III of Antioch. His reign marked significant developments in the Kingdom of Jerusalem and the Crusader states during a turbulent period filled with conflicts and political maneuvering.\n\n*Highlighted section part 1*\n**Early Life and Family Background**\nRaymond III was born around the year 1140. His father Raymond II was a powerful figure in the Levant and had played a key role in the politics of the Crusader states. Raymond III's mother Hodierna was a member of the prominent House of Hauteville. This lineage gave Raymond a strong claim to both noble status and political influence within the region. His family ties stretched deeply into the leadership of the Crusader states and made him a significant figure even before his official ascension to power.\n\nRaymond inherited the County of Tripoli after the death of his father in 1199. By that time Rome had gained authority over the region through Pope Innocent III. This new papal influence affected Raymond’s governance and his interactions with neighboring regions. His brother Bohemond IV became Prince of Antioch which further expanded the family’s political sphere.\n\n*Highlighted section part 2*\n**Reign and Political Contributions**\nRaymond III’s rule coincided with the turbulent years following the Third Crusade and the eventual fall of Jerusalem to Saladin in 1187. His principality faced ongoing military threats from the Ayyubids and internal political strife. Raymond worked to maintain stability within Tripoli while navigating complex alliances and rivalries. He was known for his cautious diplomacy often seeking to avoid direct conflict with the Ayyubid dynasty led by Saladin’s successors.\n\nOne of Raymond’s notable achievements was his cooperation with his uncle Bohemond III of Antioch and later with Bohemond IV. Their combined efforts aimed at defending the Crusader states from Muslim advances. Raymond also had complicated relationships with the Kingdom of Cyprus which was ruled by his cousin Aimery of Lusignan and later by Hugh I of Cyprus. His county remained a critical buffer zone between the Islamic worlds and the Crusader states.\n\nRaymond’s diplomacy extended to interactions with Europe as well. He maintained ties with European powers particularly those involved in the crusading efforts. These connections were vital for securing additional support and reinforcements against the growing threats from the Muslim world.\n\n*Highlighted section part 3*\n**Military Conflicts and Legacy**\nDespite his diplomatic efforts Raymond III was not spared from military confrontations. He led his forces in several battles against the Ayyubid rulers most notably against Al-Adil I and Al-Kamil who were successors of Saladin. Raymond’s forces were involved in skirmishes along the borders of his county and occasionally in campaigns supporting other Crusader states.\n\nInternal power struggles also marked his reign. There were tensions between Raymond and some of the local barons who resisted his authority. These domestic challenges required Raymond to balance between maintaining control and negotiating with the nobility.\n\nRaymond III died in 1216 leaving a complex political legacy. His county continued to be an important part of the Crusader states although it eventually fell to the Mamluks in the late 13th century. His rule had helped preserve the region’s stability for a time but the broader trends of the era ultimately led to the decline of the Crusader states.\n\nRaymond III’s story reflects the larger narrative of the Crusades where diplomacy military prowess and family influence were crucial for survival. His actions and decisions during his reign illustrate the challenges faced by the Crusader leaders as they sought to maintain their territories in the face of overwhelming odds.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.06853077560663223}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -0.00012003655137959868}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.006313735153526068}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -2.544949769973755}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.48784077167510986}, {"token": " count", "logprob": -1.0956640243530273}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.0616188645362854}, {"token": " Tripoli", "logprob": -0.12964384257793427}, {"token": " who", "logprob": -0.6508122682571411}, {"token": " ruled", "logprob": -0.23375381529331207}, {"token": " during", "logprob": -1.8362572193145752}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.2774104177951813}, {"token": " late", "logprob": -1.0517643690109253}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.060284942388534546}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -9.846202738117427e-05}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -0.025917891412973404}, {"token": "th", "logprob": -4.3987260141875595e-05}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -0.2137845903635025}, {"token": " early", "logprob": -0.0011336335446685553}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.00024780540843494236}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -7.629365427419543e-06}, {"token": "3", "logprob": -0.00031144535751082003}, {"token": "th", "logprob": -0.00018010901112575084}, {"token": " centuries", "logprob": -0.004109510220587254}, {"token": ".", "logprob": -1.113852858543396}, {"token": " He", "logprob": -0.3918720483779907}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.7439903616905212}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.6358511447906494}, {"token": " son", "logprob": -0.853010356426239}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.016290292143821716}, {"token": " Raymond", "logprob": -0.21697920560836792}, {"token": " II", "logprob": -0.0036406919825822115}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -0.6015480160713196}, {"token": " his", "logprob": -2.119736671447754}, {"token": " wife", "logprob": -0.13160133361816406}, {"token": " Hod", "logprob": -0.277004212141037}, {"toke